    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d449">

                <pb facs="0607" n="513" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg513"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.1731/19: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Thurston</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-06-09T16:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 9, 1924—4
                                p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>69. Your 38, March 15, 11 a.m. You are requested to inform the Minister of
                    Foreign Affairs that this Government is prepared to enter <pb facs="0608"
                        n="514" xml:id="pg514"/> into a <hi rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi>,
                    to be effected by an exchange of notes. Upon assurance of a reply, <hi
                        rendition="#italic">mutatis mutandis</hi>, in the same terms, you are
                    authorized to present to the Government of Nicaragua the following note:</p>
                <p>[Here follows text of note which, as presented by Mr. Thurston on June 11, is
                    printed <hi rendition="#italic">infra.</hi>]</p>
                <p>The reply of the Government of Nicaragua should set forth the same statements,
                    and should Nicaragua ask for alterations in the purport of the notes, you should
                    telegraph for instructions. The Department desires that a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi> be concluded as promptly as
                    possible.</p>
                <p>Under Section 4 Article I of the Financial Plan<anchor n="24" xml:id="d449-f1"/>
                    an arrangement of the nature proposed would apparently require the approval of
                    the bankers and the High Commission. The Department has informally ascertained,
                    however, that the bankers will raise no objection to the conclusion of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi> when their opinion is asked by the
                    Nicaraguan Government, and it is prepared to suggest to the American members of
                    the High Commission that they give their approval to the proposed arrangement if
                    the Nicaraguan Government makes the same suggestion to its representatives on
                    the Commission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d449-f1" n="24" xml:id="d449-f1-content">
                <p>A financial agreement made in 1920 between the Government of Nicaragua and the
                    two New York banking houses of Brown Brothers &amp; Co. and J. W. Seligman and
                    Co.</p>
